Asa was the son of Jehoshaphat. And Jehoshaphat was the son of Joram. And Joram was the son of Uzziah.
Uzziah begat Jotham. Jotham begat Ahaz. Ahaz begat Hezekiah.
Hezekiah is the father of Manasseh. And Manasseh is the father of Amon. And Amon is the father of Josiah.
Josiah was the father of Jehoiachin and his brothers. And in that time, the Jews were forced to go to Babylon.
And after this, the family line continued. Jeconiah was the father of Shealtiel. And Shealtiel was the father of Zerubbabel.
Zarubbabel is the father of Abiud. And Abiud is the father of Eliakim. And Eliakim is the father of Azor.
Azur was Bouh Zadok's father. Zadok was Bouh Akim's father. Akim was Bouh Eliud's father.
Elioenai was the father of Eleazar. Eleazar was the father of Mattan. Mattan was the father of Jacob.
Jacob was the father of Joseph. Joseph was the husband of Mary. And Mary gave birth to Jesus, who is called the Christ.
Therefore, there were 14 generations from Abraham to David. And there were 14 generations from David until the Jews were forced to leave for Babylon. And there were 14 generations from that time until Christ. Jesus Christ was born
This is how the birth of Jesus Christ came about. His mother Mary was engaged to Joseph. Before they lived together, it was discovered that she was pregnant. She was carrying the Son of the Holy Spirit.
Her husband, Youssef, was a deeply religious man. He didn't want to publicly shame her. He decided to quietly divorce her.
But while Joseph was pondering these things, an angel of the Lord appeared to him in a dream. And the angel said, "Joseph son of David, do not be afraid to take Mary as your wife, for the child who is conceived in her is from the Holy Spirit."
She is going to give birth to a son. And you must name him Jesus. That is why, he is going to save his people from their sins.
All this happened so that what the Lord had said, what was to happen, could come to pass. He had said through the prophet:
The virgin woman will conceive and give birth to a son, and they will call him Immanuel. (Isaiah 7:14). The name Immanuel means "God with us."
Joseph woke up and did what the angel of the Lord had commanded him. He took Mary into his home and married her.
But he did not consummate the marriage until she gave birth to a son, and he named him Joseph Jesus.
The Magi's visit to Christ.
Jesus was born in Bethlehem of Judea. This took place at a time when Herod was king of Judea. After Jesus was born, wise men from the east came to Jerusalem.
They said, "Where is the child who was born to be king of the Jews? For we have seen his star when it rose, and we have come to worship him."
And when King Herod heard this news, he was very upset. And all the people who were in Jerusalem were also upset.
Herod summoned all the chief priests of the people, and also the teachers of the law. He asked them, "Where is the Messiah to be born?"
They answered them, "In Bethlehem of Judea, for thus it is written through the prophet, 'But you, Bethlehem, in the land of Judah, are not the least among the rulers of Judah; for out of you will come a ruler who will shepherd my people Israel.'"'"
" 'But you, Bethlehem Ephrathah, small among the clans of Judah, from you shall come forth for me one who will be ruler over Israel, whose origin is from of old, from ancient times.' " (Micah 5:2)
And after that, Herod secretly called the wise men and asked them the exact time when the star appeared.
And he sent them to the city of Bethlehem and said to them: "Go and search diligently for the child, and when you find him, bring me word so that I may also go and worship him."
After the wise men had heard what the king had said, they went on their way. The star which they had seen in the east went before them, until at last it stopped over the place where the child was.
And when they saw the star, they were very happy.
The wise men went to the house. And there they saw the child with his mother Mary. They prostrated to him and worshipped him. And after that, they opened their treasures. And they gave him gold, frankincense, and myrrh.
But God warned them in a dream not to return to Herod. So they returned to their own country by another route. The flight of the Holy Family into Egypt
And when the wise men had gone, Joseph had a dream. In the dream, an angel of the Lord appeared to him. The angel said to him, "Get up! Take the child and his mother and flee to Egypt. Stay there until I tell you to return. Herod is going to look for the child. He wants to kill him."
Youssef got up. And at night, he left for Egypt with the child and his mother Maryam.
And they remained there until the death of King Herod. And so the word spoken by the Lord through the prophet was fulfilled: "When Israel was a child, I loved him, and from Egypt I called my son" (Hosea 11: 1).
And Herod understood that the wise men had deceived him. And he became very angry. And he gave orders concerning Bethlehem and the surrounding areas. And he said that they should kill all the children who were two years old and under. And this was at the same time that the wise men saw the star.
And in this way, the words spoken by the prophet Jeremiah were fulfilled. He had said:
A voice was heard in Ramah, loud weeping and great mourning. Rachel weeping for her children, refusing to be comforted because they are gone. (Jeremiah 31:15) Jesus' family returns to Nazareth.
After Herod died, Joseph saw a dream while he was in Egypt. In the dream, an angel of the Lord appeared to him.
The angel said, "Rise! Take the child and his mother and go to the land of Israel, for those who were trying to kill the child are dead."
And so Joseph got up and took the boy and his mother Mary and brought them back to the land of Israel.
Now then, it has been heard that Archelaus was king of Judea. Archelaus was ruling in place of his father Herod. This is what made Joseph afraid to go there. Instead, Joseph returned to the land of Galilee after receiving a warning in a dream.
And he lived there in a city called Nazareth. And in this way, the prophecies that the prophets spoke about Jesus were fulfilled. They said, "So that he will be called a Nazarene."
John the Baptist is preparing the way.
At that time, John the Baptist came and began to preach in the Judean wilderness.
He said, "Turn away from your sins! The Kingdom of Heaven is near."
John is the one about whom the prophet Isaiah spoke. He said, "A voice is calling out in the desert: 'Prepare the way for the Lord. Make his paths straight.'" (Isaiah 40:3)
John's belongings were made of camel hair. He wore a leather belt on his waist. He ate locusts and wild honey.
And people came to him from Jerusalem and all the Judean regions, and they were coming like this from all the regions which are around the Jordan River.
And when they confessed their sins, John baptized them in the river Jordan.
John saw many Pharisees and Sadducees coming to the place where he was baptizing. He said to them, "You brood of vipers! Who warned you to flee from the coming wrath? For I tell you, God is able from these stones to raise up children to Abraham." And the crowds asked him, "What should we do then?" He answered, "Whoever has two tunics should share with the person who has none. And whoever has food should do the same." And when he had spoken, a great crowd came to him, and he was by the sea. And a great crowd came to him, and he told them, "I tell you the truth, among those born of women there has not risen anyone greater than John the Baptist. Yet even the least in the kingdom of heaven is greater than he." From the days of John the Baptist until now, the kingdom of heaven has been forcefully advancing, and forceful men are seizing it by force. For all the prophets and the law prophesied until John. And if you are willing to accept it, this is Elijah who is to come. I tell you, Elijah has come already, and they did not recognize him, but did to him everything they wished. So also the Son of Man will surely suffer at their hands." And he said, "The kingdom of God is not coming with signs to be observed, nor will they say, 'Look, here it is!' or 'There!' for indeed, the kingdom of God is within you." And he said to the disciples, "The harvest is plentiful, but the laborers are few; therefore pray earnestly to the Lord of the harvest to send out laborers into his harvest."
Bring forth fruit meet for repentance.
Do not think that you can say among yourselves, "Abraham is our father." I tell you, God is able from these very stones to raise up children for Abraham.
The axe is already at the roots of the trees. All the trees that do not produce good fruit will be cut down. They will be thrown into the fire.
We baptize you with water, and we call upon you to turn away from your sins. But after me, one will come who is mightier than I, and I am not worthy to carry his sandals. He will baptize you with the Holy Spirit and with fire.
Here is the translation: "He has the fork in his hand to clean the hay from his threshing floor. To gather his wheat into the granary. But to burn the chaff with unquenchable fire." Jesus is baptized
Jesus came from Galilee to the River Jordan. He wanted to be baptized by John.
But John tried to prevent him. And he said to Jesus, "I need to be baptized by you, and how can you come to me?"
Jesus answered: "Let it be so now, for it is proper for us to do this, because by doing this we are carrying out the holy plan of God." John agreed.
And after Jesus was baptized, he came up out of the water. And at that time the heavens were opened. And Jesus saw the Spirit of God descending like a dove and alighting on him.
A voice from heaven said, "This is my son, whom I love; I am well pleased with him."
Jesus is tempted.
The Holy Spirit led Jesus into the desert. And there, Satan tempted him.
After forty days and forty nights of fasting, Jesus became hungry.
And the experienced one came to him and said to him, "If you are the Son of God, command this stone to become bread."
Jesus answered, "It is written: 'Man shall not live by bread alone, but by every word that comes from the mouth of God'" (Deuteronomy 8:3).
And after that, the devil took Jesus to the holy city and stood him on the highest point of the temple.
"If you are the Son of God," he said, "throw yourself down. For it is written: 'He will command his angels concerning you, to guard you carefully. They will carry you in their arms, so that you will not strike your foot against a stone.' (Psalm 91:11, 12)
Jesus answered him, "It is written, 'Man shall not live by bread alone,'" (Deuteronomy 6: 16).
And finally, the Devil took Jesus to a very high mountain. And he showed him all the kingdoms of the world and their glory.
"If you prostrate to me and worship me," he said, "then I will give you all of this."
Jesus said to him, "Get behind me, Satan! For it is written: 'You shall worship the Lord your God, and Him alone you shall serve.'" (Deuteronomy 6:13)
And after that, the devil left Jesus. And the angels came and took care of him. Jesus began to preach.
John was in prison, and when Jesus heard about this, he returned to Galilee.
Jesus left Nazareth and went to live in the town of Capernaum. This town was by the lake, in the area of Zebulun and Naphtali.
And in this, it was fulfilled that the prophet Isaiah had said. He said:
Land of Zebulun! Land of Naphtali! Galilee, where the non-Jewish people live! The land on the Mediterranean Sea! The region east of the Jordan River!
The people who are living now in the darkness will see a great light. They are now living in a very dark country. But light will break upon them." (Isaiah 9:1, 2)
And from that time, Jesus began to preach. He said, "Repent of your sins! The Kingdom of Heaven is near." Jesus Chooses the First Disciples
Jesus was walking one day by the Sea of Galilee. And there he saw two brothers. They were Simon Peter and his brother Andrew. They were casting a net into the lake. They were fishermen.
Jesus said, "Come, follow Me, and I will make you fishers of people."
And immediately they threw open their window and hurried after him.
And after he left from there, he saw another two brothers. These are James the son of Zebedee and his brother John. They were in a boat with their father Zebedee. And as they were preparing their nets, Jesus called them.
And immediately they left the boat and their father and followed Jesus. Jesus heals the sick.
Jesus walked all over Galilee, teaching in the churches and preaching the good news, which is the kingdom of God. And he healed all the diseases and ills that were among the people.
The news spread of him throughout all of Syria. People brought to him all who were sick with various diseases. Some were suffering from great pains. Others were being controlled by demons. Some were shaking violently. Others could not move at all. And Jesus healed everyone.
A large crowd followed him. There were people from Galilee, from the region they called the Decapolis, and from Jerusalem and Judea. And there were other people who came from the region that is on the other side of the Jordan River.
Jesus bestows blessings.
Jesus saw the crowds, so he went up the mountain and sat down. And his disciples came to him.
And then he started teaching them. He said:
Blessed are those who are spiritually needy. The Kingdom of Heaven is theirs.
Blessed are those who mourn, for the Lord will comfort them.
Blessed are the meek, for they shall inherit the earth.
Blessed are those who hunger and thirst for righteousness, for they will be filled.
Blessed are the merciful, for they will be shown mercy by God.
Blessed are the pure in heart. They will see God.
Blessed are the peacemakers, for they will be called children of God.
Blessed are those who suffer, for they will be given what is right. The Kingdom of Heaven belongs to them.
You are blessed when people laugh at you and hurt you for my sake. And you are blessed too when people invent all kinds of evil lies against you for my sake.
Be happy and pleased. Your reward in heaven is great. People did the same thing and harmed the prophets who lived before their time. Salt and light.
You are the salt of the earth. But if the salt loses its saltiness, how can it be made salty again? It is no longer good for anything, except to be thrown out and trampled under people's feet.
You are the light of the world. A city built on a hill cannot be hidden.
Zada, people don't light a candle and put it under a bowl. But they put it on top of a holder. And this way it lights up for everyone who is in the house.
"In the same way, let your light shine before others. Then they will see the good things you do. And they will praise your Father in heaven. Jesus gives full meaning to the law."
Do not suppose that I have come to abolish the Law or the Prophets; I have not come to abolish them, but to fulfill them.
What I am going to tell you is true. Heaven and earth will pass away before the smallest mark of the law is made to disappear. Not the smallest part of a pen can be made to disappear from the law before everything is completed.
Do not break even one of God's commandments, not even in a small way. And do not teach people to break them. If you do this, you will be called the least important people in the Kingdom of Heaven. But if you obey these commandments and teach them to people, you will be called important people in the Kingdom of Heaven.
This is what I tell you. You must be more righteous than the Pharisees and the teachers of the law. If you are not like this, you cannot enter the kingdom of heaven. Murder
Have you heard what they said to the people who lived long ago? They told them, "You shall not murder" (Exodus 20:13). Whoever murders will be liable for that.
But I say to you: Do not be angry with your brothers; anyone who is angry with his brother will be brought before the court. And anyone who says to his brother, 'You good-for-nothing!' will be brought before the Sanhedrin. And anyone who says to his brother, 'You faithless Christian!' is liable to be thrown into the fiery hell of Gehenna.
Imagine we find the one to whom you are offering your sacrifice on the altar, and you remember that your brother has something against you.
Put your gift down before the altar. But first, go and be reconciled to your brother, and then come back and offer your gift.
If we assume that someone has a lawsuit against you and takes you to court, try to resolve the issue quickly. Do this while you are still together. If you don't do this, he may hand you over to the judge. The judge may hand you over to the police. And you may end up in jail.
What I'm going to tell you is true. Don't leave until you've paid the last millime! Adultery.
Have you not heard the one who said, 'Do not commit adultery'? (Exodus 20:14).
But I say to you: Do not set your hearts on anything as an object of lust, for whoever sets his heart on anything as an object of lust, his mind has already committed adultery with that object.
If your right eye causes you to sin, tear it out and throw it away. Your eye is only a part of your body. It is better to lose it than to have your whole body thrown into hell.
If your right hand is causing you to sin, cut it off and throw it away. Your hand is only a part of your body. It is better to lose it than to have your whole body go to hell. Divorce.
It was said, 'When a man divorces his wife, he must give her a certificate of divorce' (Deuteronomy 24:1).
But I say to you: Whoever divorces his wife causes her to commit adultery, and whoever marries a divorced woman commits adultery. A man is not to divorce his wife unless she proves unfaithful. Oaths
And again, you have heard what was said to your people long ago, for they were told, "Do not break the covenant that you made with the Lord your God, and do not break the oaths that you swore to Him."
As for me, this is what I tell you: Do not make any promises of this kind at all. Do not make them in the name of heaven. For that is the throne of God.
Do not make them in the name of the earth. That is the seat on which the feet of God are placed. Do not make them in the name of Jerusalem. That is the city of the great King.
And don't swear by your heads. You cannot turn even a single hair white or black.
Let your "yes" mean "yes," and your "no" mean "no." Anything more than this is from the devil. Be kind to other people.
Have you heard what he said: "An eye for an eye and a tooth for a tooth" (Exodus 21: 24, Leviticus 24: 20, Deuteronomy 19: 21).
As for me, this is what I tell you: Do not resist the evil person. If someone strikes you on the right cheek, turn to him your left cheek also.
Imagine that someone takes you to court to take your shirt from you. Let him take your jacket from you as well.
If someone is forcing you to go one mile, go with them two miles.
Give to anyone who asks something of you. Do not refuse to lend something to anyone who wants to borrow something from you. Love your enemies.
Have you heard what some people said: "Love your neighbor" (Leviticus 19:18) and "Hate your enemies."
But I say to you, Love your enemies, and pray for those who persecute you.
And at that time, you will become the children of your father in heaven." He causes the sun to rise on people who do good and on people who do evil. He sends rain on people who do right and on people who do not do right.
If you love those who love you, what will you gain? Even tax collectors do that.
If you were delivering, it was to your people. What did you do that others didn't? Even infidels do this.
So, be perfect, just as your heavenly Father is perfect.
Helping people in need.
Beware, so that you do not do good deeds in front of people. Do not do it so that people can see you. If you do this, your Father who is in heaven will not give you a reward.
"So that you give to the needy, do not announce this and do not sound the trumpet. Do not be like the people who pretend to be holy. They announce what they do in churches and in the streets so that people honor them. What I am going to tell you is true: they have received their full reward."
In order to give to the needy, do not let your left hand know what your right hand is doing.
And after that, the charity you give should be in secret. Your Lord will reward you; He sees what you do in secret. Prayer.
Just as you pray, do not be like those who play at being religious. They love to stand and pray in synagogues and street corners. They love for people to see them. What I am about to tell you is the truth. They have already received their full reward.
Just as you pray, go into your room. Close the door and pray to your Father who cannot be seen. He will reward you. Your Father sees what you do in secret.
And if you pray, don't keep repeating and talking too much like some people who don't know God. They like to keep being heard because they talk too much.
Don't be like them. Your father knows what you need before you ask him.
This is how you should pray. Our Father who art in heaven, Hallowed be thy name.
Let your kingdom come. Let your will be done on earth as it is in heaven.
Give us today our daily bread.
Forgive our sins, just as we have also forgiven those who sinned against us.
We are protected from falling into sin when we are tempted. We are saved from the evil one.
Forgive people when they wrong you. If you do this, your Father in heaven will also forgive you.
If you do not forgive men their trespasses, neither will your Father forgive your trespasses. Fasting.
As you do not eat, you are not a gloomy person like those who represent holiness only. They keep their faces very sad. They want to show people that they are fasting. What we are going to tell you now is true. They have received their full reward.
If, however, you abstain from food, put olive oil on your head. Wash your face.
And so, other people won't know that you are fasting. Only your unseen Father knows this. He will give you the reward. Your Father sees what happens in secret. Hide your wealth in the heavens.
Don't hide treasures for yourselves on earth. Rust and corrosion can destroy them. Thieves can enter and steal them.
But instead, hide treasures for yourselves in heaven, where the moths do not eat them, nor do they rust. Where thieves do not break in, nor do they destroy houses to take them.
Your heart will be where your treasure is.
The eye is like a lamp for the body. Imagine your eyes are in good condition. Then your whole body will be filled with light.
If your eyes are bad, then your whole body will be full of darkness. If the light that is inside you is darkness, then this darkness will be great!
No one can serve two masters at the same time. They will hate one and love the other, or they will be devoted to one and despise the other. No one can serve both God and money at the same time. Don't think
"I tell you, do not be afraid. Do not be afraid for your lives, and for what you will eat or what you will drink. And do not be afraid for your bodies and for what you will wear. Is not life more than food? And is not the body more than clothing?"
Look at the birds of the air. They do not sow or gather into barns. Yet your heavenly Father feeds them. Are you not much more valuable than they?
Can you add even one hour to your life by worrying?
And why worry about clothing? Look at how wildflowers grow. They don't work or make clothing.
As for me, this is what I say to you: even Solomon in all his glory was not arrayed like one of these flowers.
If God clothes wild grass beautifully, won't He clothe you even better? After all, grass exists only for today. Tomorrow it will be gathered into fire. Your faith is too weak!
Then do not be anxious and do not say, 'What will we eat?' or 'What will we drink?' or 'What will we wear?'
People who do not fear God pant after all these things. Your Father who is in heaven knows that you need them.
But as for you, seek first the kingdom of God and his righteousness. All these things will be given to you as well.
Therefore do not worry about tomorrow, for tomorrow will worry about itself. Each day has enough trouble of its own.
Be fair when you judge others.
Do not judge others, and you will not be judged.
So that they evaluate you in the same way that you evaluate other people. So that they evaluate you in the same way that you measure other people.
You see a bit of sawdust in your friend's eye, and you don't notice the plank in your own eye.
How do you tell your friend, "Let me get some of that sawdust out of your eye"? How do you say that when you have a piece of wood in your eye?
Hypocrite! First, take the beam out of your own eye, and then you will see clearly to take the splinter out of your friend's eye.
Do not give what is holy to the dogs. Do not throw your pearls before the swine. If you do this, perhaps they will trample them underfoot. Then they may turn and tear you to pieces. Ask, seek, knock
Ask and you will receive; seek and you will find; knock and the door will be opened for you.
Everyone asks so that they may receive. The one who searches so that they may find. The door so that it may be opened to the one who knocks.
Supposing your son asked you for bread, which of you would give him a stone?
Or else, he would have been asking for a fish, so which one of you would have given him a serpent?
And even if you were evil, you know how to give your children good gifts. How much more, your heavenly Father, gives good gifts to those who ask Him!
In everything, treat others as you would like to be treated. This is what is written in the law and by the prophets. The great and small doors.
Enter the Kingdom of God through the narrow gate. The gate is wide and the road is easy that leads to death and to hell. Many people go through this road.
The door is narrow and the road that leads to life is difficult. Only a few people will find it. The tree and its fruits.
Beware of false prophets, which come to you in sheep's clothing, but inwardly they are ravening wolves.
You can know their true nature through their actions. "Do people pick grapes from thorny bushes? Do they pick figs from thistles?"
In the same way, every good tree bears good fruit. But a bad tree bears bad fruit.
A good tree does not bear bad fruit, and a bad tree does not bear good fruit.
Every tree that does not bear good fruit is cut down and thrown into the fire.
Every tree is known by its own fruit.
Not everyone who says to me, 'Lord, Lord,' will enter the kingdom of heaven. For whoever does the will of my Father in heaven, he is the one who will enter the kingdom of heaven."
Many people will say to me on that day: Lord, Lord, did we not prophesy in your name? Did we not cast out demons in your name? Did we not perform many miracles in your name?
And then we tell them very clearly: "I do not know you. Stay away from me, you who do evil!" The wise builder and the foolish builder.
And so, everyone who hears my words and puts them into practice is like a wise person who built their house on the rock.
Rain falls. Water rises. Winds blow and beat against that house. But it does not fall. It is built on the rock.
But whoever listens to my words and does not act on them is like the foolish man who built his house on the sand.
Rain falls. The water rises. The wind blows and hits that house. And it collapses with a loud noise.
And when Jesus had finished all this talk, the people were amazed at his way of teaching.
He taught as one having authority. He did not speak as the Scribes.
Jesus heals a man who was suffering from a skin disease.
Jesus came down from the mountain. A great crowd followed him.
A man who had a skin disease came and knelt before Jesus. "Lord," he said, "if you are willing, you can heal me and make me clean."
Jesus stretched out His hand and touched the man. "I am willing," He said, "to do it. Be clean!" And immediately the leprosy left him.
And Jesus said to him afterward, "Do not tell anyone. Go and show yourself to the priest. Offer the gift that Moses commanded. This will be a testimony for them." A Roman Centurion Believes
When Jesus entered Capernaum, a Roman centurion came to him. This centurion requested Jesus to help him.
He said, "Oh Lord, my servant is lying in the house and cannot move. He is suffering greatly."
Jesus said, "I will go and heal him."
The captain answered, "Lord, I am not worthy to have you enter my home. But just say the word, and my servant will be healed."
I myself am a man subject to authority. And I have soldiers who obey my orders. I say to this one, "Go," and he goes. And I say to that one, "Come," and he comes. I say to my slave, "Do this," and he does it."
And when Jesus heard this, he was astonished and said to those who followed him, "I tell you the truth, I have not found such great faith, even in Israel."
Here is the translation of the provided text: "Jesus said to the crowds, 'Truly I tell you, many prophets and righteous people longed to see what you see, and did not see it. Truly I tell you, many people will come from the east and the west, and will take their place at the feast in the kingdom of heaven. For I tell you that many prophets and righteous people longed to see what you see, and did not see it.'"
But as for those who make a claim to being among the people of the kingdom, they will be thrown out into the darkness. And there they will begin to weep and grind their teeth.
And Jesus said to the Roman leader, "Go! Everything will happen as you have believed." And at that very hour, his servant was healed. Jesus heals many people.
When Jesus came to Peter's house, he saw Peter's mother-in-law. She was lying in bed and had a fever.
Jesus touched her with his hand, and the fever left her. She got up and began to serve him.
And when night came, many people who were possessed by demons were brought to him. And he cast out the demons with a single word. And he healed all who were sick.
He acted like that to fulfill the words of the prophet Isaiah, who said: "He bore our illnesses and carried our sorrows; yet we thought he was afflicted and struck down by God" (Isaiah 53:4). Following Jesus costs a price.
Jesus saw the crowd that was around him, and he gave orders to his disciples to go to the other side of the Sea of Galilee.
And then one of the teachers of the law came and said to him, "Teacher, I will follow you wherever you go."
Jesus answered, "The foxes have dens and the birds of the air have nests, but the Son of Man has no place to lay his head."
And another disciple said to him, "Lord, let me first go and bury my father."
Jesus said to him, "Follow me, and let the dead bury their own dead." Jesus Calms a Storm
Jesus got into a boat. The disciples followed him.
Suddenly, a fierce storm came down on the lake. The waves began to beat against the boat. But Jesus was sleeping.
The pupils walked past him, and he woke up. They said to him, "Teacher, save us! We're going to drown!"
He answered them, "It is because of your little faith! Why are you so afraid?" And Jesus got up and rebuked the winds and the waves, and they became perfectly calm. There was a great calm.
The students were amazed and said, "Who is this man? Even the winds and waves obey him!" Jesus heals two men who were under the control of demons.
Jesus arrived at the other side of the lake, in the region of the Gadarenes. He met two men who were possessed by demons. They were coming from the cemetery. The two men were so fierce that no one could pass by from there.
"Son of God, what do you want from us?" they cried. "You came here to punish us before the time comes for us to be held accountable?"
Not far away, a large herd of pigs was feeding.
The demons begged Jesus, saying, "If you want to drive us out, send us into the herd of pigs."
Jesus said to them, "Go!" And the demons came out of the men and entered the pigs. The entire herd rushed down the steep hillside and plunged into the lake and drowned in the water.
And those who were guarding the pigs fled. And they went to the city and told all things. And they said to the people what happened to the men who were possessed by demons.
And after that, the whole city went out to meet Jesus. And when they saw him, they begged him to leave their region.
Jesus heals a man who could not walk.
Jesus rode in a boat. He crossed to the other side of the lake and reached his hometown.
Some men brought to Him a man who could not walk. He was lying on a mat. Jesus saw the faith that they had. He said to the man, "Do not be afraid, Son. Your sins are forgiven."
And some of the teachers of the law said to each other, "This man is speaking blasphemies! Who can forgive sins but God alone?"
Jesus knew what they were thinking, so he said to them, "Why do you think evil thoughts in your hearts?"
Which is easier to say to you: "Your sins are forgiven" or to say: "Rise and walk"?
I love you to know that the Son of Man has authority on earth to forgive sins." And after that, He spoke to the man who could not walk and said to him: "Get up, take your mat and go to your house."
The man stood up and went to his house.
And when they saw this great crowd, they were amazed at all the things they saw. And they glorified God, who had given human beings such a wonderful and powerful authority. Jesus Chooses Matthew
And as Jesus went from there, He saw a man named Matthew sitting at the tax office. And Jesus said to him, "Follow Me." And so Matthew rose and followed Him.
After that, Jesus was having dinner in the house of Matthew. And a great number of tax collectors and sinners came. And they ate with Jesus and his disciples.
The Pharisees saw this. And they asked the disciples: "Why does your teacher eat with tax collectors and sinners?"
And Jesus heard this saying, and said, "They that are whole need not a physician; but they that are sick.
Go and learn what the words are: "I desire mercy and not sacrifice" (Hosea 6:6). I did not come to make the people who think they are righteous before God to follow me. I came to make the sinners follow me." Jesus is asked about fasting.
One day, the disciples of John came to him and said to Jesus, "Neither we nor the Pharisees fast, but why do your disciples not fast?"
Jesus answered, "How can the guests of the bridegroom mourn while he is with them? The time will come when the bridegroom is taken from them; then they will fast.
People do not sew a new piece of cloth onto old clothes. The new cloth will tear away from the old cloth. And this will further enlarge the hole.
The dead girl and the suffering woman.
And while Jesus was speaking, one of the rulers came and knelt down before Jesus and said, "My daughter has just died, but come and lay your hand on her and she will live again."
Jesus rose and walked with him. And so his disciples did as well.
At that time, a woman came up behind Jesus. She had a disease that made her bleed, and she had suffered for 12 years. She touched the edge of his clothes.
"I just need to touch his clothes, and I will be healed," I thought.
Jesus returned and saw her. He said to her, "Woman, do not lose hope. Your faith has healed you." And the woman was healed at that very moment.
And when Jesus entered the ruler's house, he saw the players of the flute. And he saw the crowd making a tumult.
He said, "Go on, the girl isn't dead, she's just lying down." But they laughed at him.
After everyone had gone out, Jesus entered. He took the girl by her hand, and she stood up.
The news of what Jesus had done spread throughout the region. Jesus heals two blind men.
And after Jesus left that place, two blind men followed him. And they cried out, "Have mercy on us, O Son of David!"
And when Jesus entered the house, the blind came to him. And he asked them, "Do you believe that I can do this?" They answered him, "Yes, Lord."
Then he touched their eyes and said, "What you believe in will happen to you."
They could now see again. Jesus warned them severely: "Make sure that no one knows about this."
They went out and spread the news. They talked about it everywhere.
And when they were going out, they brought another demon-possessed man to Jesus. The man was under the power of a demon and could not speak.
When the evil spirit came out, the man spoke. And the crowd was amazed and said, "We have never seen anything like this in Israel."
The Pharisees said, "By the prince of demons he casts out demons." There were few workers then.
Jesus walked in all the cities and villages. And he taught in their synagogues. And he preached the gospel of the kingdom. And he healed every sickness and every disease.
When he saw the crowd, he was deeply concerned for them. They were like worn-out and powerless sheep without a shepherd.
And Jesus said to his disciples afterward, "The harvest is abundant, but the laborers are few.
So, ask the Lord of the harvest to send out workers into his harvest field to gather the harvest.
Jesus sends out the twelve disciples.
Jesus summoned his 12 disciples to come to him. And he gave them authority to cast out demons and to heal all diseases and ailments.
Here are the names of the twelve disciples. The first are Simon Peter and his brother Andrew. Then comes James the son of Zebedee and his brother John.
Those who followed them were Philip and Bartholomew, and also Thomas and Matthew who had been a tax collector. And also two others, James the son of Alphaeus, and Thaddaeus.
The last two are Simon the Zealot and Judas Iscariot. Judas is the one who then betrayed Jesus to his enemies.
Jesus sent these twelve out with these instructions: "Don't go among the Gentiles," he told them. "Don't enter any Samaritan town."
Go to the people of Israel, for they are like lost sheep.
As you go, preach this message: "The Kingdom of Heaven is near."
Heal the sick. Raise the dead. Let those with skin diseases be "clean" again. Drive out demons. You received this for free, so give it freely.
Do not carry gold or silver or copper in your belts.
Do not take a bag with you on the journey. Do not take extra clothes, or sandals, or a walking stick. The worker must take what he needs.
To enter a city or village, look for someone willing to receive you. Stay at the house of this person until you leave.
When you enter the house, greet the people who live there.
If this house welcomes you, give it the blessing of your peace. But if it does not welcome you, do not bless it.
Some people may not welcome you or listen to what you have to say. If they don't do that, shake the dust off your feet as you leave that house or that town.
What I am going to tell you is true. On the Day of Judgment, the fate of Sodom and Gomorrah will be less terrible than that of this city.
Here is the translation: "I am sending you out like sheep among wolves. So be wise as serpents and innocent as doves."
Beware! The men will deliver you to the local courts. They will flog you in their temples.
So that you may be presented to the governors and kings because of me. So that you may testify before them and before those who are not Jews.
If they arrest you, do not worry about how or what to say, for at that hour it will be given to you what to speak.
It is not you who will speak. Your father is the one who will speak through you.
Brothers will hand over their brothers to be killed. Fathers will hand over their children. Children will rise up against their parents and kill them.
Everyone will hate you because of me. But whoever endures to the end will be saved.
Just as people will assault you in one place, flee to another place. What I am about to tell you now is true. You will not finish going around the cities of Israel before the Son of Man comes.
The student is no better than his teacher. The servant is no better than his master.
It suffices for the student to be like his teacher. And it suffices for the servant to be like his master. If the master of the house is called Beelzebul, then how will be the state of the people living with him?
Therefore, do not be afraid of your enemies. Everything that is hidden will be revealed. Everything that is covered up will be exposed.
What I tell you in the dark, say in the light. What is whispered in your ear, shout from the rooftops.
Do not fear those who kill the body and cannot kill the soul. But rather fear the one who is able to destroy both soul and body in hell.
Do not two sparrows sell for a farthing? Yet not one of them will fall to the ground apart from your Father.
And he even counts every single hair on your head!
So don't be afraid. Your worth is greater than the worth of many birds.
And what should I do with someone who says in front of people that he knows me? I will also say in front of my father who is in heaven that I know him.
As for the person who says in front of people that he doesn't know me? I will say in front of my father who is in heaven that I don't know him.
Do not suppose that I have come to bring peace to the earth; I have not come to bring peace, but a sword.
I came to let "the children rise up against their parents. The daughters no longer obey their mothers. The daughters-in-law will rise up against their mothers-in-law.
"The enemies of the man will be from his own family" (Micah 7:6).
Whoever loves their father or mother more than me does not deserve me. Whoever loves their son or daughter more than me does not deserve me.
And whoever does not take up their cross and follow me is not worthy of me.
Whoever finds his life will lose it, and whoever loses his life for my sake will find it.
Whoever welcomes you welcomes me. And whoever welcomes me welcomes the one who sent me.
Suppose someone welcomes a prophet as a prophet. That person will receive the reward of a prophet. And suppose someone welcomes a righteous person as a righteous person. That person will receive the reward of a righteous person.
Let's assume he was giving someone until it was a glass of cold water for the little one following me. The speech that I am going to tell you about is true. That person is surely going to take a reward."
Jesus and John the Baptist
Jesus continued to teach his 12 disciples. After that, he went to teach and preach in the cities of Galilee.
John was in prison, and when he heard what Christ was doing, he sent his disciples to him.
They asked Jesus, "Are you the one who was to come? Or shall we look for someone else?"
Jesus answered, "Go back to John and tell him what you hear and see.
The blind receive their sight. The crippled walk. Those with skin diseases are healed. The deaf hear. The dead are raised. And the good news is preached to the poor.
Blessed are those who do not abandon their faith because of me.
And when John's disciples left, Jesus began to speak to the crowds about John. He said, "Why did you go into the wilderness? To see a reed shaken by the wind?
Otherwise, why did you go out to see? A man dressed in fine clothes? No. People who are dressed in fine clothes you will find in the palaces of kings.
Well then, why did you go out to see? A prophet? Yes indeed, I tell you, and more than a prophet.
He is the one foretold in the Scriptures. He said, "Behold, I send My messenger before Your face, who will prepare Your way before You" (Malachi 3:1).
What I am going to tell you is true. No one has been born who is greater than John the Baptist. But the least in importance in the kingdom of heaven is greater than he is.
From the days of John the Baptist, the kingdom of heaven has been forcefully advancing, and violent people are seizing it.
All the prophets and the law prophesied until John.
If you are ready to accept this, John is Elijah who was supposed to come.
He who has ears, let him hear.
Who can I compare the people to today? They are like children sitting in the markets, calling out to others, saying:
"We played for you on the flute. But you did not dance. We sang a funeral song. But you did not mourn."
When John came, he neither quarreled nor drank like you. And people say, "He has a jinn."
As for the Son of Man, he came eating and drinking, and you say, 'This man is a glutton and a drunkard, and a friend of tax collectors and sinners.' But wisdom is proved right by her actions." The cities that do not escape from sin...
Jesus began to speak out against the cities where He had done most of His miracles. The people who lived there did not repent of their sins. And He said:
"Woe to you, Chorazin! Woe to you, Bethsaida! If the miracles that were performed in you had been performed in Tyre and Sidon, they would have repented long ago in sackcloth and ashes."
As for me, I say to you: that Tyre and Sidon will be in a better state than you on the day of judgment.
"And you, Capernaum, will you be exalted to heaven? No! You will be brought down to Hades. If the miracles that were performed in you had been performed in Sodom, it would have remained until this day."
As for me, I tell you: the fate of Sodom on the day of reckoning will be easier than your fate." Comfort for all who are weary.
And Jesus said at that time, "I thank you, Father. You Lord of heaven and earth. You have hidden these things from the wise and learned, and revealed them to little children.
Yes, Dad. This is what you wanted.
My father gave me everything. The father is the only one who knows the son. And the only ones who know the father are the son and those whom the son chooses to introduce to him.
Come to me, all you who are weary and burdened, and I will give you rest.
Become my servants and learn from me. I am merciful and do not become arrogant. You will find comfort for your souls.
My service is easy, and my campaign is light.
Jesus is the Lord of the Sabbath.
On a Saturday, Jesus walked through the wheat fields. His disciples were hungry, so they began to pluck the heads of wheat and eat them.
The Pharisees saw this thing and said to Jesus: "Look! This thing does not happen according to the law on the Sabbath day, but your disciples do it anyway!"
Jesus answered, "Haven't you read what David did when he and his men were hungry?
And so he entered the house of God. He and his men ate the sacred bread. Only the priests were permitted to eat it.
Haven't you read the law? It says how every Saturday the priests in the temple must work on that day. But they don't consider them guilty.
We tell you that what is found here is more important than the temple.
The Holy Book says: "I desire mercy and not sacrifice" (Hosea 6:6). You do not know what these words mean. If you knew, you would not accuse the innocent.
The Son of Man is Lord of the Sabbath.
And after he came out of that place, Jesus went to their synagogue.
There was a man who had a withered and crooked hand. The Pharisees were trying to find fault with Jesus. They asked him, "Is it lawful to heal on the Sabbath day?"
He said to them, "If one of your sheep falls into a well on the Sabbath, would you not take it out and lift it up?"
The man is worth more than the sheep! Therefore, the law allows us to do good on the Sabbath day.
And after this, Jesus said to the man, "Stretch out your hand." So he stretched it out, and it was restored, just as sound as his other hand.
But the Pharisees went out and conspired to kill Jesus, the chosen servant of God.
Jesus knew all about the schemes of the Pharisees. So he left that place. And many followed him, and he healed all their sick.
He, on the other hand, had warned them not to tell anyone who he was.
This was so that the prophecy spoken by the prophet Isaiah would be fulfilled. He said:
This is my servant whom I have chosen, My beloved in whom my soul is well pleased. I will put my Spirit upon him, And he will teach the peoples about justice.
He will not argue and he will not shout. There is no one to hear his voice in the streets.
He will not break a bent branch. He will not extinguish a weak flame. He will fix everything.
The peoples will put their hope in him" (Isaiah 42: 1-4). Jesus and Beelzebul
One possessed man, who was blind and mute because of the demons, was brought to Jesus. Jesus healed him. And afterward, the man was able to speak and see.
Everyone was amazed. They said, "Is this the son of David?"
And when the Pharisees heard this, they said, "This man does not cast out demons except by Beelzebul, the ruler of the demons."
Jesus knew what they were thinking, so he said to them, "Every kingdom divided against itself is brought to desolation, and every city or house divided against itself will not stand.
If Satan drives out Satan, he is at war with himself. So how can his kingdom endure?
You say that I cast out demons by the power of Beelzebul, so by whose power do your followers cast out demons? Therefore, it is your followers who will judge you.
If I drive out demons by the finger of God, then the Kingdom of God has come upon you.
Or else, consider this. How would you break into a strong man's house and take what he possesses? You would first have to tie him up. And then you could rob his house.
Everyone who is not with me is against me. And everyone who does not gather the sheep with me scatters them.
This is what I tell you. Every sin and every evil word spoken against the Lord will be forgiven. However, the evil word spoken against the Holy Spirit will not be forgiven.
Everyone who speaks a word against the Son of Man will be forgiven. But whoever speaks against the Holy Spirit will not be forgiven, either in this age or in the age to come.
If you do a good tree, its fruits will be good. If you do a bad tree, its fruits will be bad. You can know the tree by its fruits.
O nest of poisonous vipers! How is it possible for you, who are evil, to say anything good? Your mouths speak all that is in your hearts.
The good man speaks good words. These words come from the goodness he hides in his heart. The evil man speaks evil words. These words come from the evil he hides in his heart.
As for me, this is what I say to you: on the day of reckoning, people will have to render an account for every careless word they have spoken.
With your words, you will be condemned or acquitted. The sign of Yunus, the miracle.
Some Pharisees and teachers of the law came to Jesus and said to him, "Teacher, we want to see a sign from you."
He answered them, "Evil and faithless people seek a sign! But no sign will be given to them except the sign of Jonah."
Jonah was in the belly of a large fish for three days and three nights. Such a thing will happen to the Son of Man. He will spend three days and three nights in the tomb.
The men of Nineveh will rise in the resurrection on the last day, along with the people who are now living. And the men of Nineveh will stand up and condemn these people and prove that they are guilty. The men of Nineveh repented of their sins when Jonah preached to them. And now there is someone here who is greater than Jonah.
The Queen of the South will stand on the day of judgment with the people who are living now. And she will prove that they are guilty. She came from far away quickly to hear the wisdom of Solomon. And now there is someone more important than Solomon here.
What happens when an evil spirit leaves a man? It wanders in dry places looking for a place to rest. But it does not find it.
And then he says: "I will return to the house I left." And when he gets there, he finds the house empty. The house has been cleaned and tidied up.
And after that, the evil spirit goes away and takes with it seven other spirits, even more evil than he. They enter and dwell there. And the condition of this man becomes even worse than before. That is how it will happen to the evil people of our time." The mother of Jesus and his brothers.
And while Jesus was speaking to the crowds, his mother and brothers were standing outside. And they were wanting to speak with him.
Someone told him, "Your mother and brothers are standing outside. They want to speak with you."
Jesus replied, "Who is my mother and who are my brothers?"
Jesus pointed to his disciples. And he said, "This is my mother! And these are my brothers!"
Everyone does what they want. The one in the sky is my brother or my sister or my mother.
The Farmer's Tale
And on the same day Jesus went out of the house and sat by the sea of Galilee.
A great crowd gathered around him. So he got into a boat. And he sat in it. And all the people stood on the shore.
And after that he told them many things through parables. He said, "There was a farmer who went out to sow his seed.
Scatter the seeds on the ground. Some fell on the road. The birds came and ate them.
Some seeds fell in a rocky place that didn't have much soil. The plants grew quickly because the soil wasn't deep.
When the sun came out, it burned the plants. They withered because they had no roots.
And the other seeds fell among the thorns. And as the thorns grew, they choked the plants.
And other seeds fell into good soil. And they yielded a crop thirty times, sixty times, a hundred times more than what was sown.
He who has ears, let him hear.
The students gathered around him and asked: "Why do you use stories when you talk to people?"
He answered, "You have been given an opportunity to understand the secrets of the Kingdom of Heaven. This opportunity has not been given to outsiders.
Everyone has this kind of knowledge to take more. In fact, to have a lot. If one does not have any of this kind of knowledge, even the little one has will be taken away.
This is why I use parables when I speak to people. I say: "They see, but they do not truly see. They hear, but they do not truly hear and understand."
In them are fulfilled the words of the prophet Isaiah, who said: "You will hear but never understand; you will see but never perceive what you see."
These people's hearts have become hard; they barely listen with their ears, and they have shut their eyes. If they had not done these things, they could have seen with their eyes, and heard with their ears, and understood with their hearts; and then they could have returned to the Lord, and He would have healed them. (Isaiah 6:9,10)
Your eyes are blessed because they see, and your ears are blessed because they hear.
What I am going to tell you is true. Many prophets and righteous people wished to see what you are seeing, but they did not see it. They wished to hear what you are hearing, but they did not hear it.
Listen! This is the meaning of the story of the farmer.
People hear the message about the Kingdom, but they do not understand it. Then the evil one comes. He steals what was planted in their hearts. These people are like seed sown along the path.
And as for the others, they received the seeds that fell on the rocks. These are the ones who hear the message and receive it with joy immediately.
They, however, have no roots. That is why they do not last long. They quickly leave the faith when they encounter a problem or suffer because of the message.
And as for the others, they received the seeds that fell among the thorns. These are the ones who hear the message. But then, the worries of this life and the false promises of wealth choke them. They prevent them from bearing fruit.
The others received the seeds that fell on the good soil. These are the ones who hear the message and understand it. They produce a crop that is a hundred, sixty, or thirty times more than what the farmer planted." The Story of the Weeds
Jesus told another parable to the crowds: "Thus is the kingdom of heaven: A man sowed good seeds in his field.
And since everyone was already up, his enemy came. The enemy sowed weeds among the wheat and then fled.
The wheat began to grow and form grains. At the same time, weeds appeared.
The owner's slaves came to him and said, "Master, did you not sow good seed in your field? Where did these weeds come from?"
"This is the act of an enemy," he answered. The servants asked him, "Do you want us to go and pull out the weeds?"
"No," the owner replied. "For while you are pulling up the weeds, you may also root up the wheat with them."
Let them grow side by side until harvest time. Then I will tell the workers what to do. This is what I will tell them: "First, gather the weeds. Bind them into bundles to be burned. Next, gather the wheat. Bring it to me at my barn." The tales of the mustard seed and the yeast.
Jesus told another parable to the crowds. He said, "The Kingdom of Heaven is like a mustard seed. Someone took the seed and planted it in a field.
This is the smallest seed you have. But when it grows, it becomes the largest plant in the whole garden. It becomes a tree. Birds come and rest in its branches.
Jesus told them another parable: "The kingdom of heaven is like yeast. A woman took some yeast and mixed it with a large amount of flour. The yeast worked through the whole dough."
Jesus said all these things to the crowds through parables. He did not say anything to them without speaking in a parable.
And so it came to pass, just as the prophet had spoken. He declared, "I will open my mouth in parables; I will utter hidden things from the foundation of the world." (Psalms 78:2) Jesus Explains the Parable of the Weeds
And after this, Jesus went out from the people and walked to the house. And his disciples came to him and said to him, "Explain to us the parable of the weed in the field."
He answered, "The Son of Man is the one who sowed the good seeds."
The field is the world. The good seed represents the people who belong to the kingdom. The weeds are the people who belong to the evil one.
The enemy who plants them is the devil. The harvest is the Day of Judgment. And the workers are the angels.
Noxious plants are uprooted and burned in the fire. Thus will it be on the Day of Judgment.
The Son of Man will send his angels. They will remove from his kingdom everything that causes sin. They will also get rid of everyone who does evil.
They will put them into the blazing fire. There they will cry and gnash their teeth.
And after this, the followers of God will shine like rays of sunlight in their father's kingdom. For those who have ears to hear. Tales of the hidden treasure and the pearl.
The kingdom of heaven is like a treasure hidden in a field, which a man found and hid; then he goes and sells all that he has and buys that field.
And again, Jesus spoke of the Kingdom of Heaven as a merchant looking for fine pearls.
He found someone who had something of immense value. So he went and sold everything he had. And he bought this pearl. The story of the net.
And again, the kingdom of heaven is like a net cast into the lake, and gathering fish of every kind.
And when it was filled, the fishermen pulled it ashore. Then they sat down and gathered the good fish into baskets. As for the spoiled fish, they threw it away.
This is how the Day of Resurrection will be. The angels will come. They will separate the people who did evil from the people who did good.
So that the infidels can throw the infidels into the blazing fire of hell. And there the infidels will cry and gnash their teeth.
"Do you understand all these things?" Jesus asked. "Yes" they answered.
He told them, "Every teacher of the Law who has learned about the kingdom of heaven is like a homeowner. He goes out early in the morning to find workers for his vineyard. He agreed to pay them a denarius for the day. When he went out to see the workers, he found one who had arrived only an hour ago. 'You've worked only one hour,' he said. 'Then I will give you whatever is right,' his employer said. So he told him, 'I will give you a denarius.' The worker began to protest, 'I've worked hard all day long in the scorching heat! And this one who just got here is going to get the same pay as me?' But his employer replied, 'Friend, I am not being unfair to you. We made an agreement for the standard wage. Take what is yours and go. I want to give this last one the same as I gave you. Don't you see that whatever is right is just that? There is no injustice if both the employer and the employee are happy with the deal.'" A prophet without honor
And when Jesus finished telling these parables, he left there.
He walked to his hometown, Nazareth. And he began to teach the people in their synagogue. And the people were amazed. And they said, "Where did this man get this wisdom? Where did he get the power to do these miracles?"
Isn't this the son of the carpenter? Isn't his mother's name Mary? Aren't his brothers James, Joseph, Simon, and Judas?
Where are his sisters with us? Where did this man get all these things from?
They were all displeased with him. But Jesus said to them, "A prophet is not without honor except in his own country. He did not find any honor in his own house."
What did Jesus do other than perform a few miracles there because they did not have faith.
John the Baptist was beheaded.
And at that time, Herod, the ruler of Galilee and Perea, heard about Jesus.
He told his servants, "This is John the Baptist. He has been raised from the dead, and that is why he has such power to work miracles."
Herod arrested John, bound him, and put him in prison because of Herodias. She was the wife of Herod's brother Philip.
John was telling Herod, "Don't do it as if you're taking her unlawfully."
Herod wanted to kill John, but he was afraid of the people, because they believed that John was a prophet.
On Herod's birthday, Herodias's daughter danced for Herod and his guests. And Herod was very pleased with her.
And so he promised her and swore that he would give her anything she asked for.
Her mother said to her, "What are you going to say?" And so the young girl said to Herodias, "Give me the head of John the Baptist on a large platter." "امنحيني رأس يوحنا المعمدان على طبق كبير."
The king was very upset. But he thought of his promise and his guests whom he had invited for dinner. Therefore, he asked one of his men to give her what she had asked for.
Herod ordered John's head to be cut off while he was in prison.
They brought his head in a big plate and gave it to the girl. And then she took it and gave it to her mother.
The disciples of John came and took his body and buried him. And they went away and told Jesus. Jesus gives food to five thousand people.
Jesus heard what had happened to John, and he wanted to be alone. So he got into a boat and went to a quiet place. The people heard about this and followed him on foot from the towns.
When Jesus arrived on the shore, he saw a large crowd. He was deeply moved by them. And he healed their sick.
And when the time became close to the evening, the disciples came to Him and said, "There is nothing here. The time has become late. Dismiss the crowds so that they can go and buy something to eat in the villages."
Jesus answered them, "It is not right for them to go away. You give them something to eat."
We have five loaves of bread and only two fish, like this answer them.
"Get them here to me," he said.
And after this, Jesus said to the people to sit down on the grass. He took the five loaves and the two fish. He looked up to heaven and blessed God. He broke the loaves into pieces. And after this, he gave them to the disciples. And the disciples gave them to the people.
They all ate and were satisfied. And the disciples collected twelve baskets of the leftover fragments.
About five thousand men ate. Women and children also ate. Jesus walks on water.
And immediately Jesus commanded His disciples to get into a boat, and He told them to go before Him to the other side of the Sea of Galilee, and then He dismissed the multitude.
And after dismissing them, he went up to the mountain to pray alone. And when night came, he was alone there.
The boat was already far from the shore. The waves were hitting it because the winds were blowing against it.
Early in the morning, Jesus went out to meet his disciples. And he walked on the lake.
They saw him walking on the lake and they were scared. They said: "This is a ghost!" and they screamed in fear.
And immediately Jesus called out to them, saying, "Take heart! I am, do not be afraid."
"Lord, is it you?" Peter asked. "If it is you, tell me to come to you on the water."
Jesus said, "Come." So Peter got out of the boat and walked on the water toward Jesus.
But Peter, when he saw the wind, was afraid. And beginning to sink, he cried out, "Lord, save me!"
And immediately Jesus stretched out His hand and caught him. And He said to him, "Your faith is very weak! Why did you doubt Me?"
As they climbed into the boat, the wind died down.
And after the people on the boat worshipped Jesus and said to him: "You are truly the Son of God."
And they crossed the lake and arrived at Gennesaret.
The men who had been living there recognized Jesus. They sent a message into the surrounding countryside. People brought all the sick to Jesus.
They asked him to have the sick person touch only the border of his cloak. And everyone who touched it was healed.
What makes people "unclean"?
And some Pharisees and teachers of the law came, sitting on the law from Jerusalem. And they said to him:
Why don't your students who are teaching the wise listen? Your students don't wash their hands before eating!
Jesus answered: "Why do you not obey the commands of God? You prefer to follow your own teachings!"
The Lord said, "Honor your father and your mother." (Exodus 20:12; Deuteronomy 5:16) And He also said, "Whoever curses his father or his mother, he will be put to death." (Exodus 21:17; Leviticus 20:9)
But you allow people to say to their parents, "Whatever aid you have received from us is a pure gift from God."
That is why they are not obligated to honor their parents with their gift. You nullify the word of God in order to follow your teachings.
"O hypocrites! Isaiah was right when he prophesied about you, for he said:
These people are honoring me with what they are saying. But their hearts are far from me.
Their worship of Him means nothing to me, not even anything at all. They only teach the rules of humanity. (Isaiah 29:13)
Jesus called the crowd and said to them, "Listen and understand."
What you put in your mouth doesn't dirty you. What dirties you is what comes out of your mouth.
And afterward, his disciples came to him. And they asked him, "Do you know that the Pharisees were angered when they heard these words?"
Jesus answered, "There are plants that my Father in heaven did not plant, and they will be pulled up by the roots."
Leave them alone. The Pharisees are blind guides. If a blind man leads another blind man, they will both fall into a pit.
Peter said, "Explain this to us."
"Don't you understand yet?" Jesus asked them.
Don't you see? Everything that goes into the mouth goes to the stomach. And then it leaves the body.
The words that come out of the mouth, they come from the heart. That is the speech that makes you "unclean."
Evil thoughts come from the heart. So also murder, adultery, and sexual immorality. Also theft, false testimony, and lying to others.
Here are the things that make you "unclean." However, eating without washing your hands does not make you "unclean." Faith is a woman from Canaan.
Jesus left the region of Galilee and went to the region of Tyre and Sidon.
There was a woman from Canaan who lived near Tyre and Sidon. She came to him and said, "O Lord! O son of David! Have mercy on me! My daughter is severely possessed by a wicked spirit, and she is suffering greatly."
Jesus didn't say a word. And so his disciples came to him. And they asked him, "Send her away, for she keeps crying out and wailing after us."
Jesus answered, "I was sent only to the lost sheep of Israel."
And then, the woman knelt down before him. "Master! Help me!" she said.
He replied: "It is not right to take the children's bread and give it to their dogs."
"Yes, sir," she said. "But even dogs eat the crumbs that fall from their masters' table."
And Jesus answered and said, "Woman, great is your faith! Let it be to you as you desire." And her daughter was healed at that very moment. Jesus feeds four thousand people.
Jesus walked away from there. And he walked along the Sea of Galilee. And then he went up to a mountain and sat down.
Large crowds brought him people who were blind, lame, or unable to speak. Many others also came and laid them at his feet, and he healed them.
The people were amazed at what was happening. Those who could not speak were now speaking; the crippled were healed; the lame could walk; and the blind could see. And so, the people praised God, the God of Israel.
And after that, Jesus called his disciples to come to him. He said, "I am very concerned about these people. They have been with me for three days and they have nothing to eat. I do not want to send them away hungry. If I do that, they will become very weak on their way home."
His disciples answered him, "There is nothing here. Where will we find enough bread to satisfy this huge crowd?"
"How many loaves have you?" Jesus asked. They answered him, "Seven, and a few small fish."
Jesus told the crowds to sit on the ground.
Take the seven loaves and the fish, and give thanks. Then he broke them and gave them to his disciples, and the disciples distributed the bread to the people.
All of them ate and were satisfied. And afterward the disciples gathered seven baskets full of leftover fragments.
The number of men who ate was 4000. Women and children also ate.
After Jesus had sent the crowds away, he went up into the boat and went to the district near Magadan.
They asked Jesus to perform a miracle.
The Pharisees and Sadducees came to test Jesus. And they asked him to show them a sign from heaven.
He replied, "In the evening you look at the sky. You say, 'The weather will be good. The sky is red.'"
And in the morning you say, "Today there will be a storm, the sky is red and full of clouds." You know what you see in the sky means. But you cannot understand the signs of what is happening now.
Wicked and unfaithful people seek a sign, but no sign will be given to them except the sign of Jonah." Then Jesus left them and went away. The yeast of the Pharisees and Sadducees.
The students walked to the other side of the lake. They forgot to bring bread.
Jesus said to them, "Be on your guard. Beware of the yeast of the Pharisees and Sadducees."
The students talked to each other about this topic. And they said: "He is saying this because we didn't bring bread."
Jesus knew what they were saying, so he said to them, "Your faith is very weak! Why are you talking to each other about not having bread?"
Haven't you understood yet? Don't you remember the five loaves of bread that were enough for five thousand people? Don't you remember how much of a handful of crumbs you gathered?
Did you not remember the seven loaves that were among the four thousand people? Did you not remember how many baskets of fragments you gathered?
How can you not understand? I was not speaking to you about bread. But beware of the yeast of the Pharisees and Sadducees."
And the disciples understood then that Jesus was not referring to warning them about the yeast they used for bread, but rather he was warning them about the teachings of the Pharisees and Sadducees. Peter said that Jesus is the Messiah.
Jesus walked in the region of Caesarea Philippi. And He asked His disciples there, "What do people say about the Son of Man?"
And they answered them, saying, "Some say, John the Baptist; but others, Elijah; and others, Jeremiah, or one of the prophets."
"And as for you, what do you think?" he asked. "What makes you say that it's me?"
Peter answered and said: "You are the Christ, the Son of the living God."
Jesus answered, "Blessed are you, Simon son of Jonah! For flesh and blood has not revealed this to you, but my Father who is in heaven.
This is what I will tell you. You are Peter. On this rock I will build my church. The gates of hell will not prevail against it to destroy it.
To give you the keys to the kingdom of heaven. What is bound on earth shall be bound in heaven. And what is loosed on earth shall be loosed in heaven.
And afterward, Jesus warned his disciples not to tell anyone that he was the Messiah. Jesus speaks about his coming death.
And from that time, Jesus began to explain to his disciples what was going to happen to him. He told them that he must go to Jerusalem. And that he must suffer many things from the elders and chief priests and scribes. He must be killed and on the third day rise again.
And Peter took Jesus aside and began to rebuke him. And he said to him, "God forbid, Lord! This shall never happen to you!"
Jesus turned and said to Peter, "Get behind me, Satan! You are a stumbling block to me. You do not think about the things of God, but only about the things of men."
And afterward, Jesus spoke to his disciples. He said, "If anyone wants to follow me, he must deny himself. He must take up his cross and follow me."
If he wants to save his life, he will lose it. But if he loses his life for me, he will find it.
What's the point if someone gains the whole world and loses themselves? Or what is the one thing a person can exchange for their soul?
The Son of Man will come in the glory of His Father. His angels will come with Him, and He will give to everyone according to what he has done.
What I am about to tell you is true. Some of those standing here will not die before they see the Son of Man coming in his kingdom."
Jesus' appearance changed when he appeared.
After six days, Jesus took Peter, James, and John, the brother of James, up a high mountain by themselves.
And there before them his appearance was transformed. His face shone like the sun, and his clothes became as white as light.
And at that time, Moses and Elijah appeared before them. And Moses and Elijah were talking with Jesus.
Peter said to Jesus, "Lord, it is good for us to be here. If you wish, we will make three tents; one for you, one for Moses, and one for Elijah."
And as Peter was speaking, a bright cloud overshadowed them. And a voice came from the cloud, saying, "This is my beloved Son, with whom I am well pleased. Listen to him!"
And when the students heard this, they were terribly afraid and fell face forward to the ground.
Jesus approached, touched them, and said, "Get up, do not be afraid."
And as they looked up, they saw no one but Jesus.
They came down from the mountain. And while they were going down, Jesus said to them what they must do. He said, "Do not tell anyone what you have seen. "Wait until the Son of Man is raised from the dead."
His disciples asked him, "Why do the scribes say that Elijah must come first?"
Jesus answered, "It is true. Elijah is supposed to come and make everything new again."
But I tell you: Elijah has come, and they have not recognized him, but have done to him as they pleased. So also will they do to the Son of Man."
And the disciples understood then that Jesus was speaking about John the Baptist. Jesus heals a boy who had a demon.
And when they came to the crowd, one of the men went to Jesus. And he knelt down before him.
He said, "O Lord, have mercy on my son, for he is shaking violently and suffering greatly. He often falls into the fire or into the water."
I brought it to your students, but they couldn't afford to treat it.
"O people, you have no faith and are wicked!" Jesus answered. "How much longer will I be with you? How much longer will I put up with you? Bring the child here."
Jesus commanded the unclean spirit to come out of the boy, and it came out of him. And he was healed immediately.
And after that, the disciples came to see Jesus privately and said to him, "Why could we not drive out the evil spirit?"
He answered, "Because your faith is too weak. What I will tell you is a reality. If you have faith the size of a mustard seed, it is enough. You can say to this mountain, 'Move from here to there,' and it will move. There will be nothing impossible for you."
