They met in Galilee. And Jesus said to them, "The Son of Man is to be delivered over to the hands of men."
"Look, the Son of Man is going to be betrayed. They will flog him severely and spit on him. The soldiers will mock him and dress him in a purple robe. 'Look,' they will say, 'this is the king of the Jews!' Then they will nail him to a cross. He will be in agony for six hours. At noon, darkness will fall over the whole land until three o'clock. At that moment, Jesus, with a loud cry, will say: 'My God, my God, why have you forsaken me?' Some of the people standing there will say: 'Look, he is calling for his friend, Elijah, to come and save him!' Then Jesus will speak again in a loud voice and say: 'For this very reason, I am telling you now: before the rooster crows twice, you will deny me three times.' Then Peter, the other disciples, and even the women who had come with Jesus from Galilee, will all abandon him and flee. But there was a man named Joseph from the town of Arimathea. He was a respected member of the council. He was waiting for the kingdom of God to come. Bravely, he went to Pilate and asked for Jesus' body. He wanted to give Jesus a proper burial. Pilate was surprised that Jesus was already dead. He called for the captain of the guard and asked him, 'Is it true that Jesus of Nazareth has already died? I want to make sure he is really dead before I give him back to you.' The captain of the guard confirmed that Jesus was indeed dead. Then Pilate ordered that Jesus' body be released to Joseph, who had asked for it. Joseph then prepared Jesus' body for burial. Following the Jewish tradition for burial, he wrapped Jesus' body in a linen cloth, placing aromatic spices and perfumes on the cloth. Then, carrying the stone, he rolled it in front of the entrance to the tomb where he had laid Jesus' body. He left the tomb and returned home. But the women who had come with Jesus from Galilee did not leave. After watching where Joseph had placed Jesus' body in the tomb, they returned and prepared aromatic spices and perfumes. Then, on the first day of the week, very early in the morning, they went to the tomb. They were wondering among themselves who would roll the stone away from the entrance to the tomb so they could go in. But as they approached the tomb, they saw that the stone had already been rolled away from the entrance. As they entered, they did not find the body of Jesus. While they were standing there, puzzled, two men in clothes that gleamed like lightening appeared to them. The men said to the women, 'Why do you look for the living among the dead? He is not here; he has been raised up! Remember what he told you while he was still in Galilee, saying, "The Son of Man must be delivered over to the hands of sinful men, and he will be nailed to a cross and will suffer death, but on the third day he will rise from the dead." ' Then the women remembered his words. And on the first day of the week, early in the morning, they went to the tomb, bringing the aromatic spices they had prepared. When they entered the tomb, they found the stone rolled away from the entrance. As they went in, they did not find the body of the Lord Jesus. While they were standing there, puzzled, two men in clothes that gleamed like lightening appeared to them. The men said to the women, 'Why do you look for the living among the dead? He is not here; he has been raised up! Remember what he told you while he was still in Galilee, saying, "The Son of Man must be delivered over to the hands of sinful men, and he will be nailed to a cross and will suffer death, but on the third day he will rise from the dead." ' Then the women remembered his words. And on the first day of the week, early in the morning, they went to the tomb, bringing the aromatic spices they had prepared. When they entered the tomb, they found the stone rolled away from the entrance. As they went in, they did not
Jesus and his disciples arrived at Capernaum. There, tax collectors came to Peter. And they asked him, "Does your teacher not pay the temple tax?"
"Yes, he does that," he answered. And when Peter entered the house, Jesus spoke first. "What do you think, Simon?" he asked. "About whom do the kings of the earth take taxes and fees from? Do they take it from their sons or from others?"
"From others," Peter replied. Jesus said to him, "Therefore, the children are not to pay."
But what we don't like is to anger them. So go to the lake and cast your fishing line. Take the first fish you catch. Open its mouth. There you will find the exact coin you need. Take it and give it to them as a tax on me and you.
